package cajeta.gpu.core;

/**
 * Memory ordering for a kernel-side atomic operation or scoped memory fence —
 * how strongly the op orders surrounding memory accesses. An optional, **compile-
 * time-constant** trailing argument; omitting it keeps the safe default (the
 * backend's release/acquire ordering — `AcqRel` on Vulkan, the native default
 * elsewhere). LLVM bakes the ordering into the atomic instruction at IR-build
 * time, so the value must be a literal `MemoryOrder.X`, not a runtime variable.
 *
 * Like {@link MemoryKind}, the **ordinal is the contract**: the kernel lowerer
 * maps it to an `llvm::AtomicOrdering`. Do not reorder.
 *
 * The orderings (weakest → strongest):
 *   - {@link #Relaxed} — no ordering, only atomicity (LLVM `monotonic`). The right
 *     choice for pure counters / histograms / reductions where only the final
 *     value matters; the cheapest. (A `Relaxed` *fence* is a no-op.)
 *   - {@link #Acquire} — no later access can move before this load/RMW; pairs with
 *     a `Release` to consume published data.
 *   - {@link #Release} — no earlier access can move after this store/RMW; pairs
 *     with an `Acquire` to publish data.
 *   - {@link #AcqRel} — both Acquire and Release. The default for read-modify-write.
 *   - {@link #SeqCst} — a single total order across all `SeqCst` ops. The strongest
 *     and most expensive.
 *
 * BACKENDS. CPU / AMD / NVPTX honour all five. Vulkan's memory model constrains
 * what `OpAtomic*`/`OpMemoryBarrier` accept: a device-scope atomic must carry
 * storage-class acquire/release semantics, so a `Relaxed` (or `SeqCst`) atomic is
 * raised to `AcqRel` to stay `spirv-val`-clean. This clamp applies to BOTH integer
 * and float atomics on Vulkan (see `vkClamp` in SpirvKernelLowering).
 */
public enum MemoryOrder {
    Relaxed,   // 0 — atomicity only, no ordering (monotonic)
    Acquire,   // 1 — acquire; later accesses can't hoist above
    Release,   // 2 — release; earlier accesses can't sink below
    AcqRel,    // 3 — acquire + release (the RMW/fence default)
    SeqCst     // 4 — sequentially consistent (single total order)
}
